\section{Decision Trees}\label{sec:a2:decision-trees}

The dataset \texttt{Hitters} contain data on the salary of Baseball players and additional information on years in the baseball league (\texttt{Years}) and the number of hits (\texttt{Hits}). To access the data, use:

<<echo=TRUE, eval=TRUE>>=
library(uuml)
data("Hitters")
@

We are now going to build an algorithm to grow decision trees. The algorithm used below is based on the algorithm explained in Chapter 9.2.2 in \citet{hastie2009elements}. To split a tree, see Eq. (9.12-9.14).

\begin{enumerate}
\item Create a test set by setting aside the 30 first observations. Remove those observations from your training set.
<<echo=TRUE, eval=TRUE>>=
# Remove NA values
Hitters <- Hitters[complete.cases(Hitters),]

# Create test and training set
X_test <- as.matrix(Hitters[1:30, c("Years", "Hits"), drop = FALSE])
y_test <- Hitters[1:30, c("Salary")]
X_train <- as.matrix(Hitters[31:nrow(Hitters), c("Years", "Hits"),
                             drop = FALSE])
y_train <- Hitters[31:nrow(Hitters), c("Salary")]
@

The predictor objects \texttt{X\_train}, \texttt{X\_test}, and the example predictors below are numeric matrices; the responses remain numeric vectors. When selecting rows or columns of a predictor matrix, use \texttt{drop = FALSE} to retain both dimensions, including for one observation or one predictor. For example, use \texttt{X[rows, cols, drop = FALSE]}.

\item (3p) Implement an R function to split observations binary greedily. Use the complete-case data prepared above; additional missing-value handling is not required. Here we use the \citet[][Eq. 9.12-9.14]{hastie2009elements}, but with the addition that each child node must contain at least $l$ observations. The function should take a design matrix \texttt{X}, an \texttt{y} variable and a minimal leaf size \texttt{l}. The function should return a list with the index set of the observations in \texttt{R1}, \texttt{R2}, the split value \texttt{s} and the covariate used for the split \texttt{j}, or \texttt{NULL} if no valid split exists. Below is a rough sketch how you can implement the function.

<<echo=TRUE, eval=FALSE>>=
tree_split <- function(X, y, l){
  checkmate::assert_matrix(X)
  checkmate::assert_numeric(y, len = nrow(X))
  checkmate::assert_int(l)

  # Store the sum of squares
  SS <- matrix(Inf, nrow = nrow(X), ncol = ncol(X))
  for(j in 1:ncol(X)){
    for(k in 1:nrow(X)){
      s <- X[k,j] # Choose split point
      R1 <- which(X[,j] <= s)
      R2 <- which(X[,j] > s)

      # Do if R1 or R2 is smaller than the leaf size l
      # Compute c1
      # Compute c2
      SS[k, j] <- # Compute the sum of squares
    }
  }

  # No candidate split satisfies the minimum child size.
  if(!any(is.finite(SS))) return(NULL)

  # The final results for the min SS (arg min)
  j <- # Which column was used for the split-point
  s <- # What value of X was used as the split-point
  R1 <- which(X[,j] <= s)
  R2 <- which(X[,j] > s)
  list(j = j,
       s = s,
       R1 = unname(R1),
       R2 = unname(R2),
       SS = min(SS))
}
@

\emph{Hint 1!} Several candidate splits may have the same minimum SS. Any valid minimising split is acceptable; the example code selects the first minimum:
<<echo=TRUE, eval=FALSE>>=
which(SS == min(SS), arr.ind = TRUE)[1, ]
@
Repeated predictor values are a separate issue: observations with the same value must stay together when splitting that predictor.

\emph{Hint 2!} Leave \texttt{SS} equal to \texttt{Inf} for candidates with fewer than $l$ observations in either child. If no finite score remains, \texttt{tree\_split()} must return \texttt{NULL}. Having at least $2l$ observations does not guarantee a valid split: predictors may be constant, or repeated predictor values may prevent a sufficiently balanced split. Whenever a split is \texttt{NULL}, \texttt{grow\_tree()} must create a leaf whose \texttt{gamma} is the mean response in that node, including when the root cannot split. Remove each processed node from the queue, including leaf nodes, so the loop makes progress.

See below for an example how the function should work.

<<echo = FALSE>>=
tree_split <- function(X,y,l=5){
  if(l == 5){
  list(j = 1,
       s = 5,
       R1 = c(1,  2,  3,  5, 6, 9, 13, 16, 18, 19),
       R2 = c(4,  7,  8, 10, 11, 12, 14, 15, 17, 20),
       SS = 1346633)
  } else if(l == 1){
      list(j = 2,
       s = 132,
       R1 = c(1,  2,  3,  4, 5,  6,  8,  9, 11, 12, 13, 14, 16, 17, 18, 19),
       R2 = c(7, 10, 15, 20),
       SS = 904383.4)

    }

}
@

<<echo=TRUE, eval=TRUE>>=
# Lets choose a small data to try out our algorithm with
X_check <- as.matrix(Hitters[31:50, c("Years", "Hits"), drop = FALSE])
y_check <- Hitters[31:50, c("Salary")]
# These are the names of the players we look at
rownames(Hitters)[31:50]
# This is how it should work
tree_split(X_check, y_check, l = 5)
# We can also make a split without any limit on leaf size
tree_split(X_check, y_check, l = 1)
@

\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "tree_split")|

\item (2p) Using \texttt{l=5}, report the predictor (name or column index), split value, and sum of squares (SS) for the first split of \texttt{X\_train} and \texttt{y\_train}. Obtain all three from one call to \texttt{tree\_split()}.
\item (3p) Use the function \texttt{tree\_split()} to create a function \texttt{grow\_tree()} that takes the arguments \texttt{X}, \texttt{y}, and \texttt{l} and then build a decision tree. The returned tree can be a \texttt{data.frame} that looks as below. This is just one example implementation, you are free to implement this however you want.

\begin{samepage}
\emph{Reading hint:} Revisit Section~9.2.2 in \citet{hastie2009elements}, especially the paragraph following Equation~(9.14), which explains how to extend the first split into a complete regression tree. Apply the minimum-child-size and stopping rules specified in this assignment.\par
\end{samepage}

\emph{Hint 1!} Use a list in R to store the sets of observation indices you are splitting up.

\emph{Hint 2!} The indices returned by \texttt{tree\_split()} refer to rows of the subset supplied to that call. Convert them back to row positions in the original \texttt{X}.

\begin{exampleblock}{Indices and pointers (toy case, $l=1$)}
\setlength{\parskip}{3pt}
A pending node contains original data rows 4, 9, and 15. A split returns local indices \texttt{R1 = c(1, 3)} and \texttt{R2 = 2}. Map them back as follows:
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE,results='hide'>>=
node_rows <- c(4L, 9L, 15L)
local_R1 <- c(1L, 3L)
local_R2 <- 2L
node_rows[local_R1] # Original data rows: 4, 15
node_rows[local_R2] # Original data row: 9
@
Write this left child of tree row 1 as tree row 2 and update its parent's \texttt{R1\_i}:
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
pointers <- data.frame(R1_i = c(-1L, -1L),
                       R2_i = c(-1L, -1L))
pointers$R1_i[1] <- 2L
pointers
@
Here 2 points to a \emph{tree} row; 4, 9, and 15 identify \emph{data} rows. A pointer of $-1$ marks an unwritten child. Each queue entry needs its data rows, parent tree row, and pointer to fill.

Suppose the root's right child (\texttt{other\_rows}) is already waiting. This same processing step changes the queue as follows:
\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.05}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lclcl@{}}
Stage & \shortstack{Queue\\position} & \shortstack{Original\\data rows} & \shortstack{Parent\\tree row} & \shortstack[l]{Pointer\\to fill} \\
\hline
Before & 1 & $\{4,9,15\}$ & 1 & \texttt{R1\_i} \\
       & 2 & \texttt{other\_rows} & 1 & \texttt{R2\_i} \\
\hline
After  & 1 & \texttt{other\_rows} & 1 & \texttt{R2\_i} \\
       & 2 & $\{4,15\}$ & 2 & \texttt{R1\_i} \\
       & 3 & $\{9\}$ & 2 & \texttt{R2\_i} \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Remove the processed entry and append its children with parent tree row 2. The older entry stays first. Row 2's child pointers remain $-1$ until its children are written. A processed leaf is removed without adding children.
\end{exampleblock}

Here is an template how the function could be implemented, but you are free to implement it however you want.

<<echo = TRUE, eval=FALSE>>=
grow_tree <- function(X, y, l=5){
  checkmate::assert_matrix(X)
  checkmate::assert_numeric(y, len = nrow(X))
  checkmate::assert_int(l)

  # We do an initial split
  init <- tree_split(X, y, l)
  if(is.null(init)){
    return(data.frame(j = NA, s = NA, R1_i = NA, R2_i = NA,
                      gamma = mean(y, na.rm = TRUE)))
  }
  # We use S_m to store the set of observation indicies
  # This is used to keep track of the groups of
  # observations to be split
  S_m <- list(init$R1, init$R2)
  # Storing the results
  # j = column to use for split
  # s = split point
  # R1_i = pointer to row where to go next if in R1
  # R2_i = pointer to row where to go next if in R2
  # gamma = the gamma value of the leaf (if j and s are NA)
  results <- data.frame(j = init$j,
                        s = init$s,
                        R1_i = -1,
                        R2_i = -1,
                        gamma = NA)
  while (length(S_m) > 0) {
    # As long as not all parts of the tree has been handled
    # we will either split or compute gamma
    node_split <- tree_split(X[S_m[[1]], , drop = FALSE],
                             y[S_m[[1]]], l)
    if(!is.null(node_split)){

      # Use node_split to fill j, s, R1 and R2.
      # Convert child indices to indices in the full data.

      # Add split point
      new_results <- data.frame(j = j,
                                s = s,
                                R1_i = -1,
                                R2_i = -1,
                                gamma = NA)
      results <- rbind(results,
                       new_results)
      # Add R1 and R2 to S_m
      S_m <- c(S_m, list(R1), list(R2))
      # Remove the set we just handled
      S_m[[1]] <- NULL
    } else {

      # Do stuff here to when you cant grow the tree (i.e. compute gamma)

      # Add leaf
      new_results <- data.frame(j = NA,
                                s = NA,
                                R1_i = NA,
                                R2_i = NA,
                                gamma = gamma)
      results <- rbind(results,
                       new_results)

      # Remove the set we just handled
      S_m[[1]] <- NULL
    }

  }
  return(results)
}
@

Here is an example how it should work.

<<echo = FALSE>>=
grow_tree <- function(X,y,l){
  data.frame(j = c(1, 1, 2, NA, NA, NA, NA),
             s = c(5, 3, 101, NA, NA, NA, NA),
             R1_i = c(2, 4, 6, NA, NA, NA, NA),
             R2_i = c(3, 5, 7,  NA, NA, NA, NA),
             gamma = c(NA, NA, NA, 106.5, 244.5, 509.3, 946))

}
@

<<echo=TRUE, eval=TRUE>>=
tr <- grow_tree(X_check, y_check, l = 5)
tr
@

\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "grow_tree")|

Run this check after also implementing \texttt{predict\_with\_tree()} in the next task. The check uses your growth and prediction functions together, so you can use your own tree representation.

\item (3p) Finally implement a function \texttt{predict\_with\_tree(new\_data, tree)}. Here is a starting template for the function.
<<echo = TRUE, eval=FALSE>>=
predict_with_tree <- function(new_data, tree){
  checkmate::assert_matrix(new_data)

  predictions <- numeric(nrow(new_data))

  for(i in seq_along(predictions)){
    not_in_leaf <- TRUE
    while(not_in_leaf){

      # Check the split and go to next row
      # until we end up with a gamma

    }
    predictions[i] <- predicted_value
  }
  return(predictions)
}
@

To use the tree to predict new observations $X_{new}$ as follows.

<<echo = FALSE>>=
predict_with_tree <- function(new_data, tree){
  c(106.5, 244.5)
}
@

<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
X_new <- as.matrix(Hitters[51:52, c("Years", "Hits"), drop = FALSE])
X_new
y_new <- Hitters[51:52, c("Salary")]
y_new
pred_y <- predict_with_tree(new_data = X_new, tree = tr)
pred_y
@

\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "predict_with_tree")|

\item (1p) The function \texttt{rmse(x,y)} in the \texttt{uuml} R package computes the root mean squared error between \texttt{x} and \texttt{y}.
<<echo = TRUE>>=
rmse
@

<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
rmse(x = c(75, 105, 33), y = c(102, 99, 43))
@
What is the root mean squared error (RMSE) on the test set for a tree trained on the whole training data \texttt{X\_train} and \texttt{y\_train}? Use \texttt{l=5}. Include this value in the comparison table requested in the next section.
\end{enumerate}

