\section{Running standard tools for boosting and random forests}

Use the predictor matrices \texttt{X\_train}, \texttt{X\_test} and response vectors \texttt{y\_train}, \texttt{y\_test} prepared in Section~\ref{sec:a2:decision-trees} for both models below. All three models then use the same training and test observations.

Collect the test RMSE for your single tree from the previous section, the \texttt{randomForest} model, and the XGBoost model in one comparison table with columns \emph{Model} and \emph{Test RMSE}. Add each result as you complete the tasks below. Present each RMSE once in this table and refer to it when comparing the models.

\begin{enumerate}
\item Use the \texttt{randomForest} R package to fit a random forest regression to the training data using the \texttt{randomForest} function.
<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
library(randomForest)
Hitters.rf <- randomForest(x = X_train, y = y_train)
@
\item (1p) How many variables are used at each split. Why do you get this number?
\item (1p) Use the trained random forest to predict using the \texttt{predict} function at test set and compute the RMSE of your predictions using the \texttt{rmse} function. Add the result to your comparison table.
\item (1p) Next, use the \texttt{xgboost} R package to fit a boosted regression tree model to the training data using the \texttt{xgboost} function. Use the same predictor matrices and response vectors as above.
<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
xgb <- xgboost(X_train, y_train, nrounds = 200)
@
\item (1p) Compute the test RMSE of the boosted regression tree model using the \texttt{rmse} function and add the result to your comparison table.
\item (1p) Using your comparison table, compare the test RMSEs of your single tree, random forest, and boosting model. Identify the model with the lowest test RMSE in your run and give one brief possible explanation for the differences. Answer in one or two sentences.
\end{enumerate}

