\section{*Bagged tree and random forest implementation}

\input{distinction_task.tex}

After checking your basic functions, load the optional VG checks. These use your own tree representation and do not require an exact random ensemble or test RMSE:
<<mma-assignment2-vg,echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
assignment_path <- paste0(
  "https://github.com/MansMeg/IntroML/blob/master/",
  "assignments/tests/assignment2/assignment2-vg.yml"
)
set_assignment(assignment_path)
@
Run the individual checks below as you implement each function. Once all four VG functions are ready, run \texttt{mark\_my\_assignment()} to check them together. The checks also need your basic tree functions and their helpers.


Now we can use our regression tree to do a Bagged Tree regression model. The main difference from the previous tree is that we now will draw $B$ bootstrap samples of size $N$ \emph{with replacement} from our dataset, then train $B$ trees, use them for a combined bagged prediction.

See Section 8.7 in \citet{hastie2009elements}, and especially Eq. (8.51) for details.

\begin{enumerate}
\item (1p) Implement a bagged tree regression model training function called \texttt{train\_bagged\_trees(X, y, l, B)} using the \texttt{grow\_tree()} function that return an R list of $B$ grown trees.

\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "train_bagged_trees")|

\item (1p) Implement \texttt{predict\_with\_bagged\_trees()} using \texttt{predict\_with\_tree()}. Its arguments are \texttt{new\_data}, a numeric predictor matrix, and \texttt{trees}, a list of grown trees. Return one prediction per observation by averaging across the trees.

\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "predict_with_bagged_trees")|

\item (1p) Train your bagged tree regression model on the training data and predict on the test data. Try the values $B=\{1, 10, 100, 500, 1000\}$. What is the RMSE of your predictions on the test set?

\emph{Hint!} Set a seed and train 1000 trees once. For each $B$, average predictions from the first $B$ trees. Test RMSE need not decrease every time $B$ increases, so $B=1000$ need not give the lowest RMSE in your run. The seed makes your experiment reproducible; different implementations can still produce different results with the same seed. See Figure 15.1 in \citet{hastie2009elements}.
\end{enumerate}


%  \section{*Random forest implementation}

% \input{distinction_task.tex}

Using our previous functions we can now build our own random forest regression model using our previous functions \texttt{grow\_tree()} and \texttt{predict\_with\_tree()}. Based on the bagged tree regression model, we only need to add the parameter $m$.

See Section 15.2 in \citet{hastie2009elements}, and especially Algorithm 15.1 for details.

\begin{enumerate}
\item (1p) Create a new function \texttt{grow\_tree\_m()} with the additional argument $m$. The function should grow a tree, but at every split, it should draw a sample of size $m$ of the covariates to make the split.

\emph{Hint!} At every node, including the root, sample $m$ different predictors without replacement. Search for the best split using only those predictors, drawing a fresh subset for each node. With our two predictors, $m=1$ considers one randomly selected predictor; $m=2$ considers both, as in bagging.

Let \texttt{X\_node} and \texttt{y\_node} contain the observations reaching the current node, with all predictor columns retained. The new step is:
<<feature-sampling-hint,echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
cols <- sample.int(ncol(X_node), size = m, replace = FALSE)
split <- tree_split(X_node[, cols, drop = FALSE], y_node, l)

if (!is.null(split)) {
  split$j <- cols[split$j]
}
@
If \texttt{cols = 2}, \texttt{tree\_split()} reports column 1 of its input; the tree must store column 2 of the original predictor matrix. Apply the same stopping and leaf rules as in \texttt{grow\_tree()} when the selected predictors admit no valid split.

\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "grow_tree_m")|

\enlargethispage{2\baselineskip}
\item (1p) Implement \texttt{random\_forest(X, y, l, B, m)} to train a random forest. Draw $B$ bootstrap samples, each containing \texttt{nrow(X)} observations sampled with replacement. Grow one tree per sample using \texttt{grow\_tree\_m()}, passing \texttt{l} and \texttt{m}. Return an R list of the $B$ grown trees, in the same format as \texttt{train\_bagged\_trees()}. Reuse \texttt{predict\_with\_bagged\_trees()} to average their predictions; it already calls \texttt{predict\_with\_tree()}.

Complete this scaffold by adapting your bagging training loop:
<<random-forest-scaffold,echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
random_forest <- function(X, y, l, B, m){
  trees <- vector("list", B)
  for(b in seq_len(B)){
    # Draw a bootstrap sample of observation indices.
    # Grow a tree with grow_tree_m() and store it in trees[[b]].
    # Keep predictor subsets as matrices with drop = FALSE.
  }
  return(trees)
}
@
\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "random_forest")|

\item (1p) Call \texttt{random\_forest()} on the training data with \texttt{l = 5}, $m=1$, and $B=100$, then use \texttt{predict\_with\_bagged\_trees()} to predict on the test data. Set and report the seed. Include the input settings, number of returned trees, a short extract of the predictions, and the test RMSE (using \texttt{rmse()}) in your report.

\emph{Note!} With two predictors, $m=1$ searches either \texttt{Years} or \texttt{Hits}, chosen afresh at every node, including the root; bagging searches both ($m=2$). Either method may have lower test RMSE in a run, and differences need not be small. Differences from \texttt{randomForest} may also reflect random draws, tuning settings, and implementation choices.
\end{enumerate}

